To generate a JSON object that satisfies the given schema, you can create an empty object and then add the required properties according to the schema. Here's an example of how you can do it in JavaScript:

```javascript
const schema = {
    "title": "Deactivate maintenance mode",
    "$schema": "http://json-schema.org/draft-06/schema#",
    "type": "object",
    "additionalProperties": false,
    "required": [
        "devices"
    ],
    "properties": {
        "devices": {
            "type": "array",
            "description": "List of device names",
            "items": {
                "type": "string"
            }
        }
    }
};

const data = {
    devices: []
};

for (const key in schema.properties) {
    if (schema.properties.hasOwnProperty(key)) {
        data[key] = schema.properties[key];
    }
}

for (const key of schema.required) {
    if (!data.hasOwnProperty(key)) {
        data[key] = [];
    }
}

console.log(JSON.stringify(data));
```

This script creates an empty object `data` and copies the properties from the schema to it. Then it adds the required properties (in this case, only "devices") if they don't already exist. The result is a JSON object that satisfies the given schema.

